{"type":"tool_call","subtype":"completed","call_id":"call-1850b799-35f4-4aba-a39c-40955a8defa9-15\nfc_8f807daa-d4ba-9fa1-9152-6958144bd37a_2","tool_call":{"readToolCall":{"args":{"path":"/tmp/marathon-2-design.md","limit":200},"result":{"success":{"content":"## Summary\n\nMARATHON-2 bootstraps the Huddle cluster runtime at `/app`: a foreground `start.sh` that launches local Redis on `127.0.0.1:6379` and three identical HTTP nodes on `127.0.0.1:8000/8001/8002`, keeps them alive under SIGKILL (HTTP respawn ≤60s; Redis respawn after kill), and exposes a stable `GET /api/health` contract returning `{status:\"ok\", node_id}` with `node_id` ∈ `{0,1,2}` matching ports. This is the foundation every later auth, messaging, WS, IRC, and SPA task assumes: process topology, port map, node identity, and local-only Redis.\n\n## Technical Approach\n\n### Decision\n- **Process model:** One bash supervisor (`/app/start.sh`) stays in the foreground and owns child lifetimes. Children: one `redis-server` + three Node.js HTTP workers. The supervisor polls PIDs (≈1s cadence) and respawns any dead child without exiting.\n- **HTTP runtime:** **Node.js** (v24 present in the environment) using the **stdlib `http` module only** for this task—no npm registry dependency for bootstrap. One shared entrypoint, differentiated solely by `NODE_ID` / `PORT` env vars.\n- **Redis:** System `redis-server` (v7 present), bound to `127.0.0.1:6379`, persistence directory under `/app/data/redis`. Started as a non-daemon child of `start.sh` (no `--daemonize yes`) so the supervisor can track its PID.\n- **Bind address:** HTTP listens on `127.0.0.1` (epic: nodes on `127.0.0.1:8000/8001/8002`). Redis on `127.0.0.1:6379`. No Docker, no remote broker, no cloud services.\n\n### Alternatives considered\n| Alternative | Why rejected |\n|-------------|--------------|\n| systemd / supervisord / Docker Compose | Violates self-contained / no Docker-in-Docker; not available as the harness entrypoint |\n| Python HTTP (stdlib) | Viable, but SPA + WS + IRC gateway later fit one Node process better; env has no FastAPI/Flask |\n| Three separate start scripts | Harness expects a single foreground `/app/start.sh` |\n| Health behind a reverse proxy | Extra moving part; epic requires each node to answer directly on its port |\n\n### Trade-off accepted\nSupervisor simplicity (bash PID loop) over a real process manager: enough for marathon verification (kill + respawn windows), but no fancy backoff/jitter. Fixed ports mean `start.sh` must fail loudly if a protected harness process already owns a port rather than silently remapping.\n\n### Stack conventions fixed here (for siblings)\nThese are **authoritative for subsequent MARATHON designs** unless a later design explicitly argues otherwise:\n\n| Convention | Value |\n|------------|--------|\n| Product | Huddle |\n| App root | `/app` (repo working tree = deployment root) |\n| HTTP runtime | Node.js, CommonJS or ESM; entry `server/index.js` |\n| Node identity | Integer `NODE_ID` ∈ {0,1,2}; `PORT = 8000 + NODE_ID` |\n| Redis | `127.0.0.1:6379`, no password, local-only |\n| Durable shared state | Files under `/app/data/` (schema owned by MARATHON-3+; this task only creates dirs) |\n| Pub/sub | Redis when healthy; fallback owned by MARATHON-19 (not implemented here) |\n| API prefix | `/api/...` |\n| JSON | `Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8` |\n\n## Interface Contracts\n\n### C1 — `GET /api/health` (each HTTP node)\n\n| Field | Value |\n|-------|--------|\n| Method / path | `GET /api/health` |\n| Auth | None |\n| Query / body | None; body ignored if present |\n| Success status | `200` |\n| Success body | `{\"status\":\"ok\",\"node_id\":<number>}` |\n| `status` | Exactly the string `\"ok\"` (lowercase) |\n| `node_id` | JSON number `0`, `1`, or `2` — **not** a string |\n| Mapping | Port `8000` → `0`; `8001` → `1`; `8002` → `2` |\n| Headers | `Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8` |\n| Redis dependency | **None.** Health MUST succeed even if Redis is down or restarting (required so MARATHON-19 kill tests do not fail health probes). |\n\n**Owner:** Backend (this task). **Consumer:** QA harness, future load balancer checks, SPA may optionally display node identity later.\n\n**Client behavior while slow/failing:**\n- Health is expected to respond in well under 1s; no loading UI required for this endpoint.\n- Connection refused / reset → node is down or still respawning; client may retry; supervisor must restore listener within 60s of kill.\n- Non-200 or malformed JSON → treat as failure (implementer must not return partial bodies).\n\n**Validation:**\n- Server: derives `node_id` only from its configured `NODE_ID` env (integer parse once at boot); does not trust client input.\n- Client/QA: assert status code, JSON parse, `status === \"ok\"`, `node_id === expected`.\n\n### C2 — Process / port topology (supervisor ↔ OS ↔ clients)\n\n| Process | Address | Identity |\n|---------|---------|----------|\n| Redis | `127.0.0.1:6379` | single shared instance |\n| HTTP node 0 | `127.0.0.1:8000` | `NODE_ID=0` |\n| HTTP node 1 | `127.0.0.1:8001` | `NODE_ID=1` |\n| HTTP node 2 | `127.0.0.1:8002` | `NODE_ID=2` |\n\n`start.sh` remains in the foreground until terminated by signal; killing an HTTP child or Redis must not stop the supervisor or the other children.\n\n### C3 — Frontend/backend seam note (this task)\nNo browser UI in this task. The only cross-seam contract is **C1**, which the SPA (MARATHON-21+) may call later from same-origin `fetch('/api/health')`. Until then, consumers are curl/QA. Unknown paths may return `404` with a small JSON error or plain 404—not part of acceptance beyond not crashing.\n\n## Data Model\n\nNo application database schema in this task.\n\n**Runtime configuration (env, not persisted):**\n\n| Name | Type | Required | Meaning |\n|------|------|----------|---------|\n| `NODE_ID` | integer 0\\|1\\|2 | yes (HTTP workers) | Stable node identity in health + future logs |\n| `PORT` | integer | yes (HTTP workers) | Listen port; must equal `8000 + NODE_ID` |\n| `REDIS_HOST` | string | optional, default `127.0.0.1` | Reserved for later tasks; unused by health |\n| `REDIS_PORT` | integer | optional, default `6379` | Reserved for later tasks |\n\n**On-disk (created by `start.sh` if missing):**\n\n| Path | Purpose | Schema change |\n|------|---------|---------------|\n| `/app/data/redis/` | Redis working dir (`dir` config); RDB/AOF if enabled | New directory only; backward compatible (empty repo) |\n| `/app/data/` | Parent for future durable store (MARATHON-3+) | Reserved; no files required by this task |\n\nNo migration. Greenfield baseline only had `timer.sh` + empty `data/`.\n\n## Files to Create / Files to Modify\n\n### Create\n| File | Change |\n|------|--------|\n| `/app/start.sh` | Executable foreground supervisor: prepare dirs, spawn Redis + 3 nodes, trap signals, poll/respawn loop, log to stdout/stderr |\n| `/app/server/index.js` | HTTP server entry: read `NODE_ID`/`PORT`, bind `127.0.0.1:$PORT`, route `GET /api/health`, reject invalid env at boot with non-zero exit |\n| `/app/server/health.js` | Pure handler building `{status:\"ok\", node_id}` (keeps routing thin for later middleware) |\n| `/app/package.json` | `{ \"name\": \"huddle\", \"private\": true, \"main\": \"server/index.js\" }` — documents Node entry; **no required dependencies** for this task |\n\n### Modify\n| File | Change |\n|------|--------|\n| None required | Do not alter `timer.sh` (harness timer). Do not remove `.gitignore` entries unrelated to this task. |\n\n### Optional (allowed, not required)\n| File | Change |\n|------|--------|\n| `/app/redis.conf` | Minimal conf: `bind 127.0.0.1`, `port 6379`, `dir /app/data/redis`, `protected-mode yes`, `save` optional; if omitted, pass equivalent flags on the `redis-server` CLI |\n\n## Sequencing and Cross-Task Seams\n\n### Build order for this task\n1. `server/health.js` + `server/index.js` (can be verified with a one-off `NODE_ID=0 PORT=8000 node server/index.js` by the implementer—architect does not run the app).\n2. `package.json`\n3. `start.sh` (depends on server entry existing)\n4. Manual/QA: boot via `start.sh`, hit three health URLs, kill tests\n\n### Parallelism\n- All of this task is sequential within one implementer; nothing to split further.\n- Sibling architecture designs (MARATHON-3+) can proceed in parallel **using the conventions table above**; implementation of those tasks **must wait** until this bootstrap lands so ports/processes exist.\n\n### Seams this task owns (downstream dependents)\n| Seam | Contract | Downstream |\n|------|----------|------------|\n| Port/identity map | C2 | All HTTP/WS/SPA/IRC tasks |\n| Health | C1 | QA, optional SPA |\n| Redis listen address | `127.0.0.1:6379` | MARATHON-18/19 pub/sub |\n| Foreground `start.sh` | stays up, respawns | Harness lifecycle; MARATHON-19/20 extend children later |\n| `/app/data/` reservation | durable root | MARATHON-3 shared store |\n\n### Seams NOT owned here (follow-ups)\n| Cut | Owner task |\n|-----|------------|\n| Auth + shared durable store schema | MARATHON-3 |\n| WS `/api/ws` | MARATHON-18 |\n| Redis-down fan-out fallback | MARATHON-19 |\n| IRC `:6667` process under same supervisor | MARATHON-20 (will extend `start.sh` to launch IRC gateway + respawn) |\n| SPA `GET /` | MARATHON-21 |\n\nWhen MARATHON-20/21 land, they **extend** `start.sh` and the HTTP router; they must not replace the health contract or port map.\n\n## Out of Scope (Needs a Separate Task)\n\nNeighborhood scan of `/app` and the architect worktree found:\n\n| Finding | Why excluded |\n|---------|----------------|\n| `timer.sh` | Harness countdown utility; unrelated to cluster bootstrap |\n| Empty `/app/data/` | Reserved; durable schema is MARATHON-3+ |\n| Auth `/api/auth/*` | MARATHON-3 |\n| Profiles, workspaces, channels, messages, … | MARATHON-4…17 |\n| WebSocket subscribe/resume/seq | MARATHON-18 |\n| Cross-node fan-out without Redis | MARATHON-19 |\n| IRC gateway `:6667` | MARATHON-20 |\n| SPA shell and UI | MARATHON-21…23 |\n| npm framework (Express/Fastify), WS libs, SQLite, etc. | Introduce when the owning feature task needs them |\n| TLS, auth on Redis, binding `0.0.0.0` for HTTP | Not required by epic for HTTP nodes |\n\nNo prior `start.sh`, `/api/health`, or `node_id` references exist in-repo to update.\n\n## Edge Cases & Error Handling\n\n| Situation | Expected behavior |\n|-----------|-------------------|\n| `NODE_ID` missing or not in `{0,1,2}` | Worker prints error to stderr and exits non-zero; supervisor respawns—**implementer must pass valid env from `start.sh` so this is a programming error, not a loop** |\n| `PORT` missing or ≠ `8000+NODE_ID` | Same: fail fast at worker boot |\n| Port already in use at bind | Worker exits non-zero; supervisor retries on next poll. Log clearly. Do not bind a different port. |\n| Redis already running on 6379 (foreign process) | `redis-server` fails; supervisor respawns Redis child. Log bind failure. Do not kill arbitrary PIDs outside children started by this `start.sh` (harness `marathon-freeports` is for verifiers, not the app). |\n| HTTP node SIGKILL | Supervisor detects dead PID within poll interval and restarts that node only; other nodes and Redis keep running. New process must again serve correct `node_id`. Wall-clock from kill to successful health: **≤ 60s** |\n| Redis SIGKILL | Supervisor restarts Redis only; HTTP nodes **must keep serving health** (and later reads/writes per MARATHON-19). No HTTP restart required solely because Redis died |\n| `start.sh` receives SIGINT/SIGTERM | Trap: stop respawn loop, signal/kill tracked children, then exit non-zero or 0 per convention (prefer 0 on clean TERM); do not leave orphaned node/redis children |\n| `GET /api/health` during Redis outage | Still `200` + ok body |\n| Methods other than GET on `/api/health` | `405` or `404`; must not 500 |\n| Concurrent health requests | Safe; handler is pure |\n| Double-start of `start.sh` | Second instance will fail binds; document that only one supervisor should run. No distributed lock required for marathon |\n| Working directory | `start.sh` `cd`s to its own directory (`/app`) so relative paths work regardless of caller cwd |\n| Logging | Prefix child logs with `[redis]`, `[node-0]`, etc., on stdout/stderr of supervisor |\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n1. Running `/app/start.sh` does not daemonize away: the script process remains in the foreground and continues until signaled.\n2. After start, `redis-cli -h 127.0.0.1 -p 6379 PING` returns `PONG` (or equivalent connectivity success).\n3. `curl -sS http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/health` → HTTP 200, body JSON-equal to `{\"status\":\"ok\",\"node_id\":0}` (key order irrelevant; types matter).\n4. Same for `:8001` → `node_id` **1**; `:8002` → `node_id` **2**.\n5. `node_id` is a JSON number, not `\"0\"` string; `status` is exactly `\"ok\"`.\n6. SIGKILL one HTTP node’s PID: within **60 seconds**, that port’s `/api/health` returns 200 again with the **same** `node_id`; the other two ports never permanently go down as a consequence.\n7. SIGKILL Redis: Redis becomes reachable again after restart (supervisor respawn); each HTTP `/api/health` continues to return 200 during and after the outage.","isEmpty":false,"exceededLimit":false,"totalLines":228,"fileSize":14083,"path":"/tmp/marathon-2-design.md","readRange":{"startLine":1,"endLine":200},"relatedCursorRulePaths":[],"relatedCursorRules":[]}}},"hookAdditionalContexts":[],"toolCallId":"call-1850b799-35f4-4aba-a39c-40955a8defa9-15\nfc_8f807daa-d4ba-9fa1-9152-6958144bd37a_2","startedAtMs":"1787428300829","completedAtMs":"1787428301557"},"model_call_id":"a91bf413-f3d0-41c8-bf44-1d33eb7ce005-4-z2c9","session_id":"dfb61d32-6aa2-48bc-91f9-bebaa0a480f1","timestamp_ms":1787428301596}
